WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:14.920
I will never forget this moment.

00:14.920 --> 00:20.640
A major outage had occurred for a technology company where I was responsible for all of

00:20.640 --> 00:21.920
revenue.

00:21.920 --> 00:28.480
I was new to the company, and we had impacted some of the biggest companies on the planet

00:28.480 --> 00:33.200
where they were not able to do business as promised.

00:33.200 --> 00:35.040
Everyone was scrambling.

00:35.040 --> 00:37.200
No one had a solution.

00:37.200 --> 00:40.880
And I was on vacation with my family.

00:40.880 --> 00:43.160
Here's what I learned about myself.

00:43.160 --> 00:49.580
When life gets difficult, it can be tempting to hide, to shrink, or even to just avoid

00:49.580 --> 00:52.360
the situation, to delegate.

00:52.360 --> 00:54.120
That's not what I did.

00:54.120 --> 00:56.760
And that's not what you should do either.

00:56.760 --> 01:01.380
This crisis was one of those moments where showing up really mattered.

01:01.380 --> 01:07.280
And so I did, from a hotel room, on calls I didn't plan to take, while my family waited

01:07.280 --> 01:10.360
and eventually had to go on without me.

01:10.360 --> 01:13.560
Not because I had the answers, I really didn't.

01:13.560 --> 01:18.960
I found myself hosting hourly calls with my teams, trying to create some kind of rhythm

01:18.960 --> 01:22.440
and order at a time of great uncertainty.

01:22.440 --> 01:31.360
Our customers were rightfully upset, and our teams were so nervous and needed help.

01:31.360 --> 01:35.640
Everyone needs to know at these times that someone is paying attention.

01:35.640 --> 01:39.960
That we were going to keep moving forward one step at a time, even if we didn't have

01:39.960 --> 01:41.760
it all figured out.

01:41.760 --> 01:46.900
We worked around the clock, and eventually everything was back to normal.

01:46.900 --> 01:48.560
We got through it.

01:48.560 --> 01:53.180
What I found so interesting about the outcome of the entire event is that we built stronger

01:53.180 --> 01:59.240
relationships with our clients than ever before as a result of the incident.

01:59.240 --> 02:03.720
We earned their trust by showing up when it mattered most.

02:03.720 --> 02:10.360
And here's the lesson I want you to take with you as you embark on your 90-day journey.

02:10.360 --> 02:15.620
In life, things will go wrong, and when they do, people will want answers.

02:15.620 --> 02:22.300
But if you can't give concrete answers, they expect you at a minimum to remain accessible

02:22.300 --> 02:24.500
and visible.

02:24.500 --> 02:27.820
Consistent communication will never be more important.

02:27.820 --> 02:33.100
And at the end of the day, most people understand that issues will come up.

02:33.100 --> 02:37.380
Every person and every company will have issues at some point.

02:37.380 --> 02:43.740
How you show up in those times makes all the difference, and it's what defines you as a

02:43.740 --> 02:45.060
leader.

02:45.060 --> 02:52.700
Being agile and resilient will never be more important when you're facing big challenges.

02:52.700 --> 02:55.840
So here's my question for you.

02:55.840 --> 02:58.860
How will you show up in times of uncertainty?

